arXiv:2510.06383v1 [cs.CL] 07 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pierre Lison
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mark Anderson
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Norwegian Computing Center
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Oslo, Norway
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{plison,anderson}@nr.no</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">While de-identification models can help conceal the identity of the individual(s) mentioned in a document, they fail to address <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linkage risks</span>, defined as the potential to map the de-identified text back to its source. One straightforward way to perform such linkages is to extract phrases from the de-identified document and then check their presence in the original dataset.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a method to counter search-based linkage attacks while preserving the semantic integrity of the text. The method proceeds in two steps. We first construct an <span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverted index</span> of the N-grams occurring in the document collection, making it possible to efficiently determine which N-grams appear in less than <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> documents (either alone or in combination with other N-grams). An LLM-based rewriter is then iteratively queried to reformulate those spans until linkage is no longer possible. Experimental results on a collection of court cases show that the method is able to effectively prevent search-based linkages while remaining faithful to the original content.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most documents contain personal information in one form or another. To alleviate privacy concerns and abide to data protection regulations, those texts must often be <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">de-identified</span> prior to publishing or transferring them to third parties. De-identification techniques typically operate by detecting text spans that express personally identifiable information (PII) and subsequently masking those from the document <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neamatullah et al. (2008)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lison et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Szawerna et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">De-identification techniques primarily focus on reducing the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">re-identification risk</span> – that is, the risk that an adversary might infer the identity of the individual(s) mentioned or referred to in the document based on the PIIs expressed in the text. However, according to data protection regulations such as the European GDPR, data owners also ought to mitigate the risk of <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">record linkages</span>, which happen when the de-identified data remains linkable to other data sources, and in particular to the original data collection<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          This is notably emphasised in the Opinion of the Article 29 WP on Anonymisation Techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Article 29 Data Protection Working Party (2014)</a></cite>, which highlights the <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(un)linkability</span> of pseudonymised data with its original source as one of the three criteria that anonymisation techniques should meet.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06383v1/delinking.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:295/209;" width="295" height="209"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>To prevent search-based linkages between a de-identified document and its original source, we identify all N-grams occurring less than <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times in the collection, and use an LLM to reformulate each N-gram (in its local context) until the linkages are averted.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One simple but powerful technique for establishing such linkages is <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">phrase search</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weitzenboeck et al. (2022)</a></cite>. This method entails extracting phrases from the de-identified text and then searching for their occurrence in the original document collection. As many longer N-grams will only occur once in a given corpus, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva and Cunha (2020)</a></cite>, phrase search can efficiently connect the edited texts back to their source, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a method for preventing search-based linkages through an <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverted index</span> that captures the occurrence of N-grams across the collection. This inverted index is employed to efficiently derive the N-grams – and combinations of N-grams – that are associated with fewer than <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> documents and should therefore be rephrased. An instruction-tuned LLM is then queried with the text along with this list of N-grams to edit. This process is repeated until the text no longer contains any N-gram that could uniquely identify it within the original corpus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using a dataset of court cases, we show that existing text rewriting methods are relatively poor at preventing search-based linkages. In contrast, the method proposed in this paper can drastically reduce those linkage risks while remaining truthful to the original content of the document.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is structured as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> briefly reviews related work. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Approach ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes the threat model and proposed method, which is then evaluated in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Evaluation ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Finally, Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Conclusion ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> discusses the results and concludes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Record Linkage</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The problem of record linkage is well studied in the field of data privacy, albeit primarily for tabular data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kum et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vatsalan et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Domingo-Ferrer et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gkoulalas-Divanis et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Studies of record linkage in non-tabular data are less common, although a few papers have explored it in the context of medical datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Churi and Pawar (2019)</a></cite> or social media accounts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hassanpour et al. (2023)</a></cite>. The legal implications of record linkage in unstructured data is analysed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weitzenboeck et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text de-identification</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of text de-identification is to detect spans expressing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) and subsequently mask those from the documents.
De-identification methods may rely on heuristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neamatullah et al. (2008)</a></cite>, statistical measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sánchez and Batet (2016)</a></cite>, sequence labelling models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dernoncourt et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pilán et al. (2022)</a></cite>, unsupervised methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al. (2022)</a></cite> or LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Larson et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Staab et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several studies have proposed privacy-preserving rewriting methods to obfuscate demographic indicators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2019)</a></cite>, conceal author identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao and Carpuat (2024)</a></cite>, or implement differential privacy (DP) mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igamberdiev and Habernal (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meisenbacher et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, DP-based techniques produce fully transformed texts that do not retain the document’s original content. While such transformations are suitable for tasks such as synthetic data generation, they are inadequate for many downstream uses of text de-identification – especially in legal or medical contexts where faithfulness to the source material is essential. To the best of our knowledge, this paper is the first approach specifically designed to prevent phrase-based linkage attacks while preserving the document’s semantic integrity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Paraphrasing</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A wide range of paraphrasing models have been developed, with recent progress driven by neural approaches and LLMs. These systems are typically fully automated and unsupervised <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mallinson et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wada et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witteveen and Andrews (2019)</a></cite>. A common limitation, however, is the lack of user control over which specific phrases are modified and which remain unaltered.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Approach</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first outline the threat scenario, followed by the algorithm used to identify the N-grams to rephrase and the LLM-driven rewriting procedure. Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Full example ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> provides a step-by-step example.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Threat model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on record linkage with a maximum-knowledge intruder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Domingo-Ferrer et al. (2015)</a></cite> that has access to the original collection of documents <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{d\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, given a de-identified text <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume the intruder seeks to establish a linkage between <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original text <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> by:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extracting one or more substrings <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d^{\prime}}=\{s\mid s\text{ is a contiguous sequence of tokens in }d^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is a contiguous sequence of tokens in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d^{\prime}}=\{s\mid s\text{ is a contiguous sequence of tokens in }d^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Searching for the occurrence of those strings in <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, which results in the subset <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}(S_{d^{\prime}}))=\{d\in\mathcal{D}\mid\forall s\in S_{d^{\prime}},\ s\subseteq d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(S_{d^{\prime}}))=\{d\in\mathcal{D}\mid\forall s\in S_{d^{\prime}},\ s\subseteq d\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We see the linkage as successful if <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}(S_{d^{\prime}})|&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}(S_{d^{\prime}})|&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimum threshold, as in <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-anonymity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samarati and Sweeney (1998)</a></cite>. We used <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that this paper only focuses on linkage attacks based on phrase search. While more sophisticated linkage attacks are possible, mitigating them without incurring large distortions in the semantic content of the text – and thus undermining the very aim of text de-identification – is impossible in the general case. See the Limitations section for a discussion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Extraction of N-grams to rephrase</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently determine the frequency of various N-grams (and combinations of N-grams) in a document collection <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we rely on an <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverted index</span>, a data structure that maps each word or N-gram to a list (called a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">posting list</span>) of documents it appears in. As posting lists are sorted by their document identifier, they can be efficiently intersected to derive the list of documents where a given combination of N-grams occurs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al. (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a de-identified document <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the extraction of N-grams to rephrase proceeds as follows:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extract a set <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> of N-grams occurring in it. To reduce the number of N-grams to consider, we limit the maximum N-gram length (fixed to 7 words in our experiments<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  As the frequency of N-grams decreases sharply as a function of the N-gram length <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva and Cunha (2020)</a></cite>, considering longer N-grams is usually redundant, as those would often encompass shorter N-grams that are themselves rare.</span></span></span>), and do not consider N-grams crossing sentence boundaries or <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">[REDACTED]</span> PIIs, which are per definition absent from the original text <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then sort the N-grams in <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by their length and filter out overlapping N-grams, resulting in a set <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}\subseteq S_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}\subseteq S_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the minimal set of N-grams that need to be rephrased to present search-based linkages.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each N-gram <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we finally check the nb. of documents associated with it in the index <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the length of the posting list <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, we add <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to the N-grams to rephrase.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The result of this process is a set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}\subseteq S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}\subseteq S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> of N-grams known to be occurring <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math> times in the corpus and which therefore need to be rephrased to prevent search-based linkage.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">N-gram combinations</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To extend this procedure to combinations instead of single N-grams, we can replace step 3. above with the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3b.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate the set of possible N-gram combinations <math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{m}_{S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{m}_{S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the set <math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the arity of each combination (up to a maximum arity, set to 3 in our experiments).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4b.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each combination <math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C^{m}_{S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C^{m}_{S^{\text{min}}_{d^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the posting lists of its constituting N-grams and intersect them. If the length of the intersection is <math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, we add the shortest N-gram in <math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to the set of N-grams to rephrase.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>In-context Rewriting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the N-grams <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to rephrase in a document <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are extracted, we query an instruction-tuned LLM to generate a reformulated text in which those N-grams no longer appears <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verbatim</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathsf{LLM}(d^{\prime}_{t},S^{*}_{d^{\prime}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫𝖫𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathsf{LLM}(d^{\prime}_{t},S^{*}_{d^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the LLM is provided with the local context surrounding the N-grams to be rephrased, which helps maintain fluency and contextual integration. To scale the rewriting to longer documents and ensure each LLM query is limited to rewriting a few N-grams at a time, we chunk the text in pieces of a few sentences each. We also found that instructing the LLM to generate a short chain-of-thought reasoning ahead of the rewriting led to better results. The LLM is also provided with few-shot examples of rewritten sentences. The full prompt is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Prompt ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Extraction of N-grams to rephrase ‣ 3 Approach ‣ Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, the extraction of N-grams is constrained to non-overlapping N-grams to limit the number of N-grams to consider in a single rewriting iteration. However, this means the rewritten text <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> might still contain some N-grams to rephrase (if those were ignored due to overlaps). To this end, and to account for the fact that the LLM does not always strictly follow its instructions, the rewriting procedure is repeated until the set of N-grams <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes empty.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
